WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.380
With Avengers: Infinity War set to bring together
story arcs from almost 20 Marvel movies over

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.980
the past decade, there's no denying the sheer
force and influence of the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:10.980
Universe.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.890
But it wasn't always smooth sailing.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.020
Marvel's first misstep was Iron Man 2, which
lacked the finesse fans would eventually come

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.020
to expect.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.340
Here's how one of the franchise's least-liked
movies almost derailed the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.030
Universe.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.360
Weak and clunky

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.000
One of the most scattered and unfocused films
in the MCU, Iron Man 2 is essentially two

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.750
movies crammed into one.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:36.160
We begin with Tony Stark coming to grips with
not just his newfound superhero status, but

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.750
the fact that the arc reactor in his chest
is slowly poisoning him.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.750
But wait!

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.030
There's an old diorama made by Tony's father
Howard that holds the key to a new element

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.621
that just so happens to be the exact material
Tony needs to save his life—and it just

00:00:49.621 --> 00:00:51.780
gets sillier from there.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:59.140
"It tastes like coconut… and metal."

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:05.139
Between scattered action set pieces and bizarre
side stories, there are also the easter eggs.

00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:10.810
The early days of the MCU saw the studio dropping
in nods and references that didn't feel completely

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.569
organic to the stories the films were telling.

00:01:13.569 --> 00:01:17.530
One of the biggest rotten eggs is the prototype
Captain America shield that's laying around

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.549
in Stark's workspace, which just feels like
an unnecessary Marvel geekout.

00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:24.030
Too much, too fast

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.380
Tasked with following up the surprising success
of Iron Man, the deck was arguably stacked

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.950
against Iron Man 2.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:34.600
This sequel was also the last chance of the
MCU to lay some groundwork for The Avengers.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.650
Black Widow, Nick Fury and Agent Coulson were
written into supporting roles to establish

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.900
the parts they'd eventually play in The Avengers.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.249
"I've got my eye on you."

00:01:45.249 --> 00:01:49.630
With Iron Man dealing with a personal crisis
and a bad guy, it felt forced to see Nick

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.719
Fury waving from the sidelines, trying to
shoehorn in the fact that there's a bigger

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:56.639
world out there when we hadn't even met half
of the Avengers yet.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.549
It was a classic example of show, don't tell—and
Iron Man 2 did a lot of telling.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.320
"Sir, I'm gonna have to ask you to exit the
donut."

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.679
Plans changed

00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.840
Though he's since been replaced by Taika Waititi,
Joss Whedon, and Shane Black, director Jon

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.220
Favreau helped lay the groundwork for the
MCU fans know and love today.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.220
As the director of Iron Man, he used his passion
and talent to establish a tone that would

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.110
carry on through more than a dozen subsequent
movies.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.460
Favreau also had his own ideas mapped out
for an Iron Man trilogy, planning to explore

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.829
the "Demon in a Bottle" storyline from the
comics, which focused on Stark's alcoholism.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:36.569
However, Marvel wanted a more "family-friendly"
approach.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:39.180
"There's the door!"

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:46.040
It seems Favreau may have had more storytelling
freedom in the early days—back before Marvel

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.110
realized Iron Man was their golden goose.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.959
Marvel's villain problem

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:54.239
Almost every Marvel movie has a problem when
it comes to their bad guys.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:58.180
Marvel puts great thought into its heroes,
but their evil foils often leave much to be

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.180
desired.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.400
Looking back, we can trace the origin of the
MCU's villain problems to Iron Man 2.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.870
Mickey Rourke is one heck of a talented actor,
a fact that made his one-note performance

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.760
as Ivan Vanko, a gruff Russian thug, all the
more frustrating.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.859
"I don't know if you know this, but I don't
speak Russian!"

00:03:18.859 --> 00:03:20.430
Rourke says he wanted to add

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:24.430
"...other layers and colors, not just make
this Russian a complete murderous revenging

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:25.430
bad guy."

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.849
According to him, though, a good chunk of
his performance wound up on the cutting room

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:29.849
floor.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:33.049
Considering how much Marvel was trying to
cram into this movie, it's not a surprise

00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:35.010
that Vanko's story was cut short.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.010
But it still makes you wonder what could've
been.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.500
There was also Vanko's partner, Justin Hammer.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.049
In the comics, Hammer was a legitimate corporate
rival to Tony Stark.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:47.760
In Iron Man 2, he's little more than a punchline,
leading to an awkward pairing of slapstick

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.720
and menace that was one of the weakest aspects
of an already weak film.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.720
Instead of one quality villain, Iron Man 2
gave us two subpar ones.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.500
"They're $125.7M a pop, so…

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:01.500
OH GOSH!"

00:04:02.449 --> 00:04:04.040
The sequel trap

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:09.190
When it comes to making a sequel, most studios
try to go bigger and badder, and Iron Man

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:10.780
2 fell into that same formula.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:15.790
It had more characters, more team-ups, bigger
set pieces, and wackier villains.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:20.310
Put simply: Iron Man 2 tried to go too big
too fast.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.060
Following the film's release, Robert Downey,
Jr. admitted they may have tried too hard

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.900
to make the film bigger rather than making
it good.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.500
There's little doubt that this problem was
exacerbated by all the heavy lifting to lay

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.170
the groundwork for The Avengers.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:36.010
Iron Man 2 had the deck stacked against it
from the start.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.699
Is it good?

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:41.940
Marvel Studios is at the top of Hollywood
these days, but back when Iron Man 2 arrived,

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:45.970
it was still figuring out how to make all
these big movies while keeping the quality

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:47.100
level high.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.350
Iron Man 2 hasn't aged well, either.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.350
As Collider notes:

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.140
"The film feels rushed, bloated, unsteady,
and unfocused."

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.040
"It's a perfect example of everything NOT
to do."

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.190
It still made a ton of money at the box office,
but there's no doubt some fans were turned

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:06.730
off by its convoluted plot, zany bad guys,
and excessive foundation-laying.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:10.090
Marvel learned a lot from this movie, and
these lessons likely helped Marvel make The

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:15.480
Avengers such a crowd-pleaser, but we all
had to watch this dud to get there.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.480
Thanks for watching!

